course: world
lesson: 176
title: Adverts and "8 out of 10 cats"
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: An advert is made by someone who wants your money, so it shows the best bits, hides the catch in tiny letters and picks words that sound bigger than they are.
explain: "8 out of 10 cats prefer it!" sounds huge until the small print says "based on 10 cats": ten cats, maybe the advert man's own, maybe hungry, and nobody says what the other food was. Mia's dad: "I could get 8 out of 10 cats to prefer a sock if they were hungry enough."
explain: Two questions crack most adverts: "Out of how many?" and "Compared with what?" A shampoo that makes hair "70% shinier" than not washing at all has only beaten a muddy dog.
explain: Read the small print, the "sold separately" and the "was" price, then look at what is really in the box.
explain: Laugh at the advert's trick, not at the person who believed it: grown-ups fall for these every day, which is exactly why the tricks are used.
fact: A test on only 10 cats is far too small to say what cats in general prefer; a fair test uses many animals, chosen fairly, compared with other foods.
fact: "Recommended by dentists" without saying which dentists or how many is a vague claim; dentists recommend fluoride toothpaste in general, and most brands have it.
fact: A number like "30% more fun" measures nothing real; if nobody can say how it was measured, the number is decoration.
fact: "Number one" in an advert often shrinks in the small print to number one in a tiny group, such as one kind of toy in one shop in one month.
fact: Famous people are paid to wear and use things in adverts; their skill comes from years of practice, not from the shoes.
fact: Some shops raise a price for a short time so that a later "sale" looks bigger; compare with the usual price and with other shops.
fact: If kids who take vitamin gummies are healthier, it may be because their families also eat well and exercise; going together is not the same as causing.
fact: "Zero sugar" can be true while the drink is still sweetened, coloured fizzy water; water and milk are the best drinks for children.
fact: A vote is only fair if lots of people with no reason to pick one side take part; the owner's family is not a fair jury.
fact: A fair test compares a product with other products, not with nothing, so "shinier than not washing" proves almost nothing.
fact: Adverts show everything you could buy; "sold separately" and "batteries not included" tell you what is not in the box.
fact: Words people use: "bussin'" (US kids) means really good, especially food; "mid" means just average; "fr" means "for real"; "tbh" means "to be honest"; "quid" (UK) means pounds; "lekker" (South Africa) means great or tasty; "y'all" (US South) means "you all".
exercise: A cat food advert says "8 out of 10 cats prefer it!" and the small print says "based on 10 cats". What is the main problem? | Ten cats is far too few to tell what cats in general prefer | Cats cannot taste any difference between foods | Eight out of ten is too low a score to show on TV | Adverts are not allowed to use fractions
exercise: Which two questions crack most numbers in adverts? | "Out of how many?" and "Compared with what?" | "How much does it cost?" and "What colour is it?" | "Who is on TV?" and "Is it brand new?" | "Is it in the shops?" and "Is it loud?"
exercise: A fair test of a new cat food would... | use many cats chosen fairly and compare with other foods | use the advert maker's own ten hungry cats | ask the cat owners which food smells tastiest | feed only the new food for a week and see if the cats eat it
exercise: A toothpaste advert says "Recommended by dentists!" Ollie asks, "Which dentists?" Why is that a good question? | The advert never says which dentists or how many | Dentists are not allowed to talk about toothpaste | Every dentist in the world uses that one brand | Toothpaste does not need anyone to recommend it
exercise: What do dentists really recommend? | Toothpaste with fluoride, which most brands have | Only the most expensive brand in the shop, for safety | Brushing with plain water and no toothpaste | Whichever brand is shown on TV the most
exercise: A cereal box says "Now with 30% MORE fun!" What is wrong with the number? | Fun can't be measured in percent | 30% is too small a change to notice in cereal | It should say 30% more sugar instead of fun | Only scientists are allowed to measure fun
exercise: "The number one toy in the country!" Small print: "in the category of blue plush unicorns, in March, in our shop". What has the small print done? | Shrunk "number one" down to a tiny group | Proved the toy is the best in the whole country | Shown that every shop sold out of the unicorn | Shown that the unicorn won a national prize
exercise: A famous footballer wears trainers in an advert: "Wear these and play like a pro!" Why won't they make you play like him? | His skill comes from years of training, not the shoes | Famous footballers never wear trainers in real matches at all | The trainers only work on real grass pitches | You also need to buy the matching socks
exercise: A sign says "Was £50, NOW £30!" but the price was £30 last month too. What is going on? | The "was" price was raised briefly so the sale looks bigger | The shop has really cut the usual price by £20 | Prices always go up during a sale | The toy has become £20 cheaper to make
exercise: "Kids who take our vitamin gummies are healthier!" Tom's dad asks, "Healthier than who?" What else could explain it? | Their families may also eat well and do more sport | Gummies are the only way to get any vitamins | Only healthy kids are allowed to buy gummies | Vitamins from sweets always work faster than vitamins in food
exercise: Mia finds a "Zero sugar!" drink full of sweetener and colouring and says, "So it's healthy!" What is true? | No sugar doesn't make a drink healthy; read the whole label | Every drink without sugar is good for you | Sweetener is exactly the same thing as sugar | Zero sugar also means zero colouring
exercise: Which drinks are best for children? | Water and milk | Zero-sugar fizzy drinks | Energy drinks with vitamins | Squash with sweetener
exercise: "Voted best pizza in town!" The voters were the owner's family. Why doesn't it count? | The voters had a reason to pick that restaurant | Families are not allowed to vote for anything | Pizza can never be voted on by anyone | A vote only counts when more than three people take part
exercise: "Clinically proven to make hair 70% shinier!" Small print: "a test of 12 people, compared with not washing". What is the catch? | It was compared with not washing at all | Twelve people is the perfect size for any test | Hair cannot get shinier from any shampoo | Shampoo is never tested on real people
exercise: A TV advert shows a car racing round a huge track with loops. The box has one car and a short bit of track: "Track sold separately." What did the advert show? | Everything you could buy, not what is in the box | Exactly what you get when you open the box | A toy that is not sold in any shop | The car that the shop gives away for free with every track
exercise: What does "sold separately" on a toy box mean? | You have to buy that part on its own | It comes free in a separate box | It is already inside, in a separate bag | The shop will post it to you later
test: Jayden: "8 outta 10 cats, no cap, this cat food is bussin'." The small print says it was 10 cats. What do you tell him? | Ten cats is a tiny test; ask out of how many and compared with what | He's right; 8 out of 10 is a huge majority of cats | Cat food can't be tested on cats at all | It must be true, because it was shown on TV
test: Nan: "Seen the sign, pet? Was £50, now £30. Proper bargain!" It was £30 last month. Which is right? | Not a real sale; the usual price was £30 | It's a bargain, because she saves £20 on it | It's a bargain, because sales are always real | It's a bargain only if she buys two of them
test: What would happen if the cat food maker tested 1,000 cats chosen at random, against other foods, and 8 out of 10 still chose it? | The claim would be much more believable | It would prove no more than the 10 cats did | It would prove that every cat likes it | It would be against the rules for adverts
test: "These trainers? It's giving pro footballer. I'ma play like him fr." What is true? | Trainers that fit help, but skill comes from practice | He will play like a pro from his first game | Only the footballer's own pair would work like that for him | The shoes work only after you watch the advert
test: "Shampoo's clinically proven 70% shinier innit, it says so on the bottle." The small print: 12 people, compared with not washing. What do you say? | It only beat not washing, so it proves almost nothing | "Clinically proven" means it's the best shampoo | Twelve people is more than enough for any test | 70% shinier means it works on 70% of people who use it
test: In a cartoon a kid opens a toy box and a giant loop-the-loop track jumps out, just like in the advert. Put the cartoon right. | Real boxes often hold far less; read "sold separately" | Real boxes always hold everything shown in the advert on TV | Adverts are not allowed to show any extra parts | The track arrives when you post the box back
test: Auntie in Cape Town: "Zero sugar, so it's lekker AND healthy, hey?" What is right? | Zero sugar can still mean sweetened, coloured fizzy water | It's healthy, because there is no sugar in it | Sweetener makes a drink count as fruit | Every fizzy drink is healthy if it tastes lekker
test: Teen, bored: "Best pizza in town, voted by his nan and three uncles. The pizza's mid, tbh." What is wrong with the vote? | The voters weren't fair judges | Four votes is the right number for a town | Nans are not allowed to vote on pizza | No pizza can ever be voted best
test: Dad, grumpy: "Kids who take 'em are healthier, it says. Healthier than who, eh?" Why is it a good question? | Without a fair comparison you can't tell what made them healthier | Because vitamins always make children ill | Because only grown-ups can become healthier | Because gummies are the only reason kids are healthy
test: A boy sneers: "Your mum fell for the was-£50 sign, she's so thick." What is a fair way to laugh about it? | Laugh at the sign's trick, not at the person it fooled | Laugh at her loudly so she never trusts a shop sign again | Tell everyone in class she can't count | Pretend the trick wasn't there at all
test: Leo: "30% more fun?? Did they bring a fun-o-meter into the factory lol." Is Leo right to laugh? | Yes; fun can't be measured, so the number is decoration | No; scientists measure fun in percent every day | No; it means there are 30% more flakes inside every single box | Yes, because it should really say 50% more fun
test: A cousin from Texas: "Dentists recommend it, y'all, so it's the only toothpaste worth buyin'." What is true? | Most fluoride toothpastes work well; one brand isn't special | Only that one brand protects teeth from holes | Dentists are paid by every toothpaste maker | Toothpaste doesn't work unless a dentist names it
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the assertion "eight out of ten cats prefer it", where the small print discloses a sample of ten cats? | The sample is too small for any general conclusion | The assertion is proven for all domestic cats everywhere | The proportion is too low to be reported in an advertisement | The assertion does not depend upon the number of cats tested
exam: Identify the principal deficiency of a shampoo trial in which the product was compared with no washing whatsoever. | The comparison was made against nothing rather than against other products | The number of participants was excessively large | Shininess cannot be observed by the human eye | The shampoo was not applied to the participants' hair
exam: It is given that an article was priced at thirty pounds during the preceding month and is presently advertised as "Was £50, now £30". Select the response consistent with the established facts. | No genuine reduction relative to the usual price has occurred | A genuine reduction of twenty pounds from the usual price has occurred | The usual price was fifty pounds throughout | The article has been reduced by one half
exam: Ascertain the significance of the words "sold separately" where they appear upon the packaging of a toy. | The item so described is not contained within the package | The item is supplied free of charge in a separate package | The item is contained within a sealed inner bag | The item shall be dispatched by post without charge
